  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Public Financial Management</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Maldives):  Prior to the COVID-19 pandemic, Maldives, driven by a strong tourism sector, made significant strides in its economic development. Due to the economic impacts of COVID-19 and poorly planned and managed capital investments, Maldives now faces growing fiscal and debt risks.Through the Public Finance Management (PFM) activity, USAID supports improved budget management, public investment management, and domestic resource mobilization. This activity supports the Ministry of Finance to better manage public expenditure (with a performance-based budgeting system); improve public investment decisions; conduct high-quality macro-fiscal analysis, and conduct the incidence of taxes (i.e. a study on the effects of tax policies on prices and the distribution of utilities/welfare). The PFM activity also supports the government to streamline processes and adopt international best practices to reduce corruption. FY 2022 funding will allow PFM Maldives to continue these activities, reducing the risk of a debt crisis and of the country&apos;s vulnerability to coercive economic behavior by the People&apos;s Republic of China (PRC).Through the PFM activity, USAID advances the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Objective 2.1: Strengthened public sector capacity to implement and sustain reforms that align with international norms, improve transparency, mitigate corruption, and increase opportunities for responsible international investment..</narrative>
  </description>
